<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alaska</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alaska is a large region in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. It was colonised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> from the eighteenth century, mainly for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, and administered through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> sold it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaska.jpg" alt="Alaska" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benny Benson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alaska.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alaska lies at the northwest corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, very near to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s Yukon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> to the east, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> to the south, the Bering Sea, Bering Strait and Chukchi Sea to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arctic-ocean">Arctic Ocean</a> to the north. About 500 miles of Canadian territory separate it from Washington. In the Bering Strait the Russian Big Diomede Island and the Alaskan Little Diomede Island are only 3 miles apart. Its coastline is longer than that of all the other U.S. states combined. The name derives from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleut">Aleut</a> idiom, alaxsxaq, meaning 'the mainland'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Indigenous peoples occupied Alaska for thousands of years before European contact, and linguistic and DNA studies support the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> by way of the Bering land bridge. At the Upward Sun River site the remains of a six-week-old infant belonged to a population genetically separate from other Native groups, which the archaeologist Ben Potter named Ancient Beringian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlingit">Tlingit</a> and Haida lived in the southeast, and all three of these peoples and others of the Pacific Northwest Coast suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> outbreaks from the late 18th century, the worst in the 1830s and 1860s.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> began to colonise the area in the 18th century, establishing Russian America. The expense and difficulty of holding it led to its sale to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1867 for US$7.2 million. Alaska became a territory on 11 May 1912 and the 49th state on 3 January 1959.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Alaska is a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with Juneau as its capital and Anchorage as its most populous city. Juneau is on the mainland but has no road connection to the rest of the North American highway system. More than half the state is federally owned, in national forests, national parks and wildlife refuges. The Alaska Native Heritage Center celebrates the state's 11 cultural groups.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alaska” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Russian Empire's expansion across northern Asia brought Siberia, Central Asia, the Caucasus and, until 1867, <b>Alaska</b> under a single political authority and changed the Eurasian geopolitical order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alaska</b> and Hawaii became the 49th and 50th states in 1959, and the country also holds territories including Puerto Rico and Guam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>A girl of about nine, buried near Utqiaġvik in <b>Alaska</b> around 1200 CE, had been born with a congenital defect and could never walk, yet her family had cared for her all her life.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Navajo and Apache are believed to have migrated from northwestern Canada and eastern <b>Alaska</b>, where most Athabaskan speakers live.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Some Sámi emigrated to <b>Alaska</b> in 1898 and to Newfoundland in 1907–1908 at the request of the American government, to teach reindeer herding, and an estimated 30,000 people in North America are Sámi or of Sámi descent.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>He excavated sites on Baffin Island and around north-western Hudson Bay and argued, correctly, that they were the remains of a whaling culture that had begun in <b>Alaska</b> and moved into Arctic Canada about a thousand years earlier.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thule-people">Thule people · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
